WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.909 align:start position:0%
 
in the last 24 hours 963  people have been evacuated from kabul on  the royal air force airbridge  that's a significant acceleration on the  numbers over the previous 24 hours and  reflects i think that we're now fully up  to speed in kabul uh with military  border force and foreign office consular  staff all now up and running uh and  we're looking forward to delivering  similar numbers today tomorrow and over  the days ahead so on that basis how long  will the uk evacuation plan last  well i mean we don't have it in our  gifts to say that it will last for  another  five days 10 days 15 days it is clearly  uh circumstances that are very dynamic  uh we are simply doing our best to  maximize flow each and every day but as  my colleague the defense secretary has  sadly had to say on a number of  occasions the reality is that eventually  the air bridge will have to close and  quite possibly not everybody will have  been got out that's what keeps us awake  at night that's what's motivating to us  to work as hard as we are to ensure that  those numbers are the absolute minimum  i mean what what does other people do  who aren't at the airport at the moment  one of our correspondents has been  talking to the sahiri family from london  who 13 of them went over there for for a  wedding they're in kabul they can't at  the moment get to the airport  what are the options for people like  that  so i think i'd say three things if i may  firstly uh  our understanding is that actually the  taliban are not  uh turning people away uh where they  have done i've heard that it's more that  they are being officious rather than  malicious  and so if people are called forward for  flights they should have confidence that  the taliban will let them through uh to  the handling center we know that the  crowds outside the handling center have  been a barrier for many uh yesterday we  reconfigured uh the handling center uh  did some military engineering to try to  ease those cruel control challenges  which hopefully will increase flow and  then the final thing i would say is that  people should not despair that the air  bridge is their one route out of  afghanistan to the united kingdom there  will be a second phase to this operation  which will see people being able to  enroll at other british embassies and  high commissions in the region and to be  able to be resettled that way and so  even when the air bridge inevitably has  to close that's not the end of people's  chances of getting to the uk  i mean the reports that the contract  staff who protected the british embassy  are now getting no protection from great  britain labor calls that shameful  that's got to be looked at hasn't it  well if you're referring to the gaada  world staff who protect the embassy i  can tell you that they  have arrived at the airport this morning  and we'll be moving them out later today  so they they are now fully covered  yes yes um in terms of of getting people  out that we have relied on  during our time in afghanistan i mean  why did the foreign secretary not take  our responsibility  to get interpreters out of afghanistan  seriously enough to take a phone call  well i don't know the exact details of  foreign office ministers call sheets  what i can tell you as the secretary of  state said yesterday  i know that no one phone call would have  been decisive in changing the trajectory  either for the collapse of the afghan  government or indeed for the  acceleration of the airlift what i'm  focused on is not recrimination over who  should and shouldn't have done what when  i'm focused  every single minute of every single day  in making sure that the military  operation to extract people from kabul  goes as well as it possibly can and that  we leave as few people behind as  possible but if you're focused on that  every minute of every day you'd expect  your colleagues to to do the same  wouldn't you if the foreign secretary  wouldn't make a phone call or take a  phone call and it's passed on to his  junior and that phone call still doesn't  take place if the defense secretary um  said to as he said we knew that the  afghan government was melting like ice  on friday and yet the prime minister  then heads off on holiday on saturday i  mean it does raise questions doesn't it  about the commitment of your colleagues  i can only tell you what i see in the  meetings and phone calls that i have uh  all the way through every day have done  for the last week will do for the next  one and what i see is that from the  prime minister to secretaries of state  to my junior ministerial colleagues  around government to senior civil  servants all the way down to the brave  volunteer civil servants who have gone  forward to cabal from the border force  from the mod and from the foreign office  is people across the majesties  government working their backsides off  in order to get people out i completely  understand why the media wants to  ask these sorts of questions it's  entirely reasonable but you'll forgive  me perhaps if i focus on the job at hand  and hopefully be reassured if i tell you  that absolutely every colleague at every  level be they political military or  civil service is doing everything they  possibly can to make sure that we get as  many people out as quickly as we  possibly can i mean you served you've  got a better understanding of this and  certainly a lot of your colleagues who  haven't served and done a tour of duty  in  afghanistan everyone's talking about  being blindsided by this and certainly  it was a very rapid  operation by the taliban but um  it's emerged in a report submitted to  the government in january it laid out  how what sort of risks the u.s  withdrawal posed to all of this so  there's been eight months of planning  time  so why has the uk government let's  forget about every other country why is  the uk government been blindsided by  this  well i don't think i accept that  characterization of what's happened over  the last week absolutely we've been  blindsided by the speed of the collapse  of the afghan government and the speed  at which the taliban have been able to  take control but  there was a plan for the drawdown of  british troops in afghanistan called op  catalon that has been in place since the  beginning of last year because the doha  agreement that president trump signed  meant that on the first of may this year  in theory we were going to be leaving  and so we've had a plan for that all  along likewise op pitting which is the  evacuation of entitled personnel and  those who have worked alongside us  that's the plan of long-standing so both  of the plans that have been executed  over the last few months and are in  progress right now in the terms of  pitting have long since been devised and  we're ready to go the bit that's caught  us out and i don't think anybody uh can  uh  deny this is that everything happened  far more quickly than we ever thought it  would  um  as a  a veteran of afghanistan what did you  make of general cynic carter chief of  the defense staff who said on this  program a couple of days ago the taliban  are country lads with a a moral code not  our enemy  i'm obviously  not quoting him directly there but those  are the words he said what impact does  that have on veterans what impact does  it have on the families of the  457  what impact does it have on serving  armed forces personnel  well i think that  actually to veterans and to serving  personnel um we're probably the audience  that gets what cvs was saying more than  anybody else because  you know the three operational campaigns  that i served in northern ireland iraq  and two tours to afghanistan in northern  ireland by the end people like jerry  adams and martin mcginnis who had been  in the ira were a part of government  in iraq people like mukhtar al-sadr who  was the leader  forgive me forgive me minister for  jumping in but we are a bit tight for  time look i've been in contact with a  lot of members of the armed forces over  the past few days they're not on board  with what the chief of the defense staff  was saying they're absolutely furious  morale is at rock bottom and you only  have to look at what was posted on  forces tv on when they played out our  interview on on instagram and look at  the hundreds of comments below to see if  people are not on board with this  whether they're currently serving or  veterans they're furious  well i think you you should let me make  the point that i was making which is  that we get that when we go off to fight  we are  a means to an end but we are not the end  in itself and that very often the piece  that follows that is imperfect and just  as members of the ira are now in  government in northern ireland the  people who are leading the insurgency  that we were fighting in iraq are now  the key power brokers for a government  that we're working with in baghdad it  was always inevitable that the taliban  would be part of the solution in  afghanistan it makes me sick of course  it does to see the scenes that we're  seeing in afghanistan i gave some of the  best years of my life and i risked my  life  in afghanistan but when i was patrolling  in sangin in 2009 or in kabul in 2005 i  was doing my best to make a difference  in those six months to the lives of the  people that lived in those communities  what's happening now is for politicians  to answer for but the fact that the  taliban are part of the piece as painful  as that is for people like me who have  served it was always going to be the  reality  what would you say though to the mother  of a young soldier who lost their lives  in afghanistan you know i don't know  eight years ago  when they hear  cynic carter say these are country lads  with a moral code  but i have been at many military  funerals i have held the hand of  bereaved mothers when they've been  presented with their elizabeth cross  i have already many times had to look  people in the eye and reassure them that  the sacrifice that their son made was  not in vain i don't think that anything  that has happened over the last week in  any way detracts from what our brave  service men and women did in those  extraordinary years whilst we were in  hellmann and my message to veterans  to the bereaved families of those who  never came home is that they should  continue to take pride in the bravery  and the sacrifice of their loved ones  because they did an amazing job whilst  we were in afghanistan nothing can ever  take that away from them politicians  will be held to account for the outcome  but nothing diminishes  the bravery the courage the  determination the professionalism and  too many cases the sacrifice that our  people put in during those difficult  years in helmand  minister thanks for your time  thank you

